Day 1 Assignment – Spark Ecosystem / Architecture
1. What is Apache Spark?

Apache Spark is an open-source distributed data processing framework designed for fast and large-scale data processing. It was developed at the University of California, Berkeley, and later became an Apache Software Foundation project.

Spark processes massive datasets across multiple machines in parallel and supports:

Batch Processing
Real-Time Stream Processing
Machine Learning
Graph Processing
Interactive SQL Queries
Key Features
In-memory computation
Fault tolerance
Distributed processing
High performance
Multi-language support (Python, Scala, Java, R)
Example

Instead of processing 1 TB of data on a single machine, Spark distributes the data across multiple nodes and processes them simultaneously.

2. Why Apache Spark When We Have Hadoop?
Hadoop MapReduce Limitations
Hadoop MapReduce	Apache Spark
Disk-based processing	In-memory processing
Slower execution	Faster execution
Complex coding	Easy APIs
Separate tools needed	Unified framework
Not suitable for iterative ML	Excellent for ML
Why Spark?

Spark stores intermediate data in memory instead of writing to disk repeatedly.

Example

Machine Learning algorithms require multiple iterations.

Hadoop writes results to disk after every iteration.
Spark keeps data in memory.

Result:

Hadoop → Slow
Spark → Up to 100x faster
Spark + Hadoop

Spark does not replace Hadoop completely.

Common architecture:

HDFS → Storage

Spark → Processing

